answers={
'AMBC-01':'Trade activity attracts capital, but useful investment must improve reliability, financing and customer service. Ownership changes, new funding and operating cash benefits occur through different routes and at different stages.',
'AMBC-02':'Credit helps when total cost and repayment dates fit a verified operating cycle. Clear borrower records, collection evidence and disciplined follow-up matter alongside the lender’s ownership and funding.',
'IIDF-01':'An ETF combines exposure, domicile, share class, trading currency and exchange. A structure improves the investor’s outcome only when its relevant tax and recurring-cost advantages exceed its implementation costs.',
'IIDF-02':'Residence and transaction dates determine which income, gains, accounts and reporting rules must be examined. Preserve records across the move and fund tax obligations separately from family support and investing.',
'IIDF-03':'A usable payment route requires eligible accounts, supported currencies and a workable recipient channel. Compare delivered cash, then give support, reserves and investment contributions their own amounts and dates.',
'KTHF-01':'Spendable cash follows actual payroll and household payments, not taxable pay alone. Dated payslips and cash commitments make the monthly plan testable when deductions, income or policy change.',
'KTHF-02':'Contributions, timing, fees, inflation and return paths shape retirement purchasing power. Conditional scenarios reveal sensitivity; current liquidity and a later withdrawal plan remain separate decisions.',
'KTHF-03':'Insurance covers defined claims under its terms. Accessible reserves fund household payments, timing gaps and lost income; the policy and the cash plan must be read together.',
'KTHF-04':'A useful housing comparison includes entry cash, loan payments, maintenance, exit debt and the same rental horizon. Stress the full household budget when rates or income change.',
'KECA-01':'Compare net distributions or maturity gains with the cash actually paid, using consistent periods. Cash generation, reinvestment and sector capital needs determine whether distributions can continue.',
'KECA-02':'A defensible value range makes cash generation, reinvestment, growth and required returns explicit. Sector-specific claims and sensitivity analysis explain what can change the estimate.',
'KECA-03':'Weights, shared exposures and concentration shape portfolio risk. Whole-share quantities, costs and liquidity then connect the allocation policy to an affordable order plan.',
'KECA-04':'Contributions, inflation and return order affect purchasing power and goal attainment. A conditional simulation helps compare choices; it does not promise a future outcome.',
'USMPC-01':'The economic claim, benchmark and ownership vehicle answer different questions. Define the investor’s job for the money before combining them into a portfolio.',
'USMPC-02':'What the investor retains depends on recurring costs, execution, tax and funding together. A low stated fee helps only within an exposure and ownership structure that fits the investor.',
'USMPC-03':'The same underlying exposure can produce different outcomes through residence, domicile, withholding, custody and currency. Preserve the investor’s dated facts and document the entire route.',
'USMPC-04':'A portfolio architecture assigns jobs and limits to each sleeve. It becomes useful when risk budgets, funding and governance constrain later security choices.',
'USMPC-05':'A broad core and a growth fund can form a deliberate tilt rather than independent diversification. Look-through weights and a review rule make that intention visible.',
'USMPC-06':'A thematic fund adds concentrated industry risk even when it holds multiple companies. Size the position against a portfolio loss budget and explicit thesis conditions.',
'USMPC-07':'Option premium is compensation for an obligation. A tactical sleeve needs suitable underlying ownership, sufficient collateral and a complete downside-and-assignment plan.',
'USMPC-08':'Separate sleeves form one policy only when overlap, covariance, collateral, costs and rebalancing are considered together. A governance rule makes the portfolio maintainable.',
'USMPC-09':'Contribution and withdrawal timing connect investments to the household. Sequence tests, accessible reserves and scheduled reviews help keep the portfolio useful when life changes.',
'NSE-DG-01':'A staged contribution plan must fit fees, whole-share quantities and residual cash. Dividend assessment then asks whether the underlying business can sustain the payment.',
'NSE-DG-02':'A watchlist broadens research when each candidate has an evidence requirement and a portfolio role. A deferred decision can be the correct output.',
'LEAD-01':'Key-person risk is concentration of knowledge, relationships and authority. Resilience improves when deputies perform real work and the continuity plan is tested.'}
